WEBVTT

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:05.580
 This is Delicious Revolution, a show about food.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:20.770
 It started in my mind conceptually like an art project because, you know, by default I think it's just the way that I think is in this way that's been labeled art project because it's process-oriented.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:35.390
 What I found that was really nice for me was that there was no intimidation factor with food, was that people spoke a very everyday utilitarian language about food.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:54.190
 They expressed their satisfaction or desire or needs or wants or clear observations where I knew that these same people I had interacted with in galleries and there was some other kind of filter there then.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:01:04.410
 It wasn't an easy communication. Food is an easy communication. It's either yummy or it's not. It's either satisfying a need or it's not.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.180
 They either want more of it or they don't.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.900
 And I love that. That was really exciting to me.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:23.200
 Delicious Revolution is a show about food, culture, and place, made by Chelsea Wills and me, Devon Samson.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:33.260
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of the food movement, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists, and who have a vision for a different food system.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:41.750
 This first season of Delicious Revolution, we talk to friends who are deeply engaged with many aspects of food, and have inspired us over years with their thoughts and stories.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:49.860
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app, and you can find us at deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:02:02.000
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:03.450
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:09.180
 On September 25th, I interviewed Victoria Wagner at her studio in Santa Rosa, California.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:15.240
 Victoria is an artist, educator, and baker based in Sonoma County, California.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:21.750
 She is a member of the 428 Collective and currently teaches at California College of Art in Oakland.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:31.830
 Her work is comprised of organic, multi-layered paintings, sculptures, and drawings that vacillate between objective and non-objective notions.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:41.300
 The main thread of her work is found in tonal vibration, electricity, and naive human understanding of the simplicity of the natural world.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:53.880
 Recently, her work has been shown at Yerba Buena Center for the Arts, Southern Exposure, the Lab, Headland Center for the Arts, the Sonoma County Museum, and the De Rosa Art and Nature Preserve.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:01.870
 So, um, let's just start in the beginning of this thing.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:13.270
 Um, I just want to hear about you, um, you know, when, I guess, when did you start baking, and when did you start being an artist?

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:17.780
 And how do those things relate to each other?

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:31.100
 Baking, I, I would say that I started formally baking when I was, um, pursuing my undergraduate degree up at Humboldt State.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:40.500
 Very accidentally, in a, in a sort of paid position, I would say I fell into that in a very accidental way.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:49.400
 There was, um, unfortunately, a baker that was injured at the restaurant where I worked, and I haphazardly took over for her.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:54.020
 Um, and I just knew how to do it.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:04:04.570
 I had grown up with a mom that was a baker and a grandma that was a very, very regular, consistent, amazing baker with this Eastern European bend.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:15.980
 Um, and I always felt like there was a certain magic waiting in the freezer all the time when we went to grandma's house because it was stocked with cookies and cakes and delights and everything.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:23.730
 And she was not a believer in indulgence, but she always had this idea that a little sweet goes a long way.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.290
 So I learned a lot from her.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:38.720
 I learned a lot from my mom just watching in terms of process, but I didn't really know what I knew until I accepted that job to take over for, um, the injured coworker.

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.130
 And it kind of just went from there.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:47.030
 I was at the time pursuing a degree in painting, undergraduate degree in painting.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:51.650
 So I was at that time kind of setting up my future life.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:05:12.390
 I was painting during the times I wasn't working and I ended up kind of moving across the street, literally across the street to a pretty wonderful, small French American restaurant and started doing their pastries, their evening pastries and, um, breads.

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:27.160
 So I went from working in this small little vegetarian restaurant, doing veggie cookies and things like that, to actually becoming a dessert baker and a bread baker, um, and loved it.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:28.810
 I absolutely loved it.

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.110
 How long did you do that for?

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:35.430
 Probably three years, three, four years.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:37.970
 And then I graduated and moved to San Francisco.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:48.580
 Um, that was up at Humboldt State in Arcata, moved to San Francisco and got a job right away with sort of friends of friends.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:57.510
 That had a restaurant called Firefly in, uh, Noe Valley and became their dessert baker and did that for a year.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:13.090
 Um, and then ended up moving, moving just to, to do a house sitting job up in Sonoma County and never left and ended up working at Village Bakery, doing their pastries there.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.950
 And it's just odd little things happened along the way.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:25.910
 I was hired by this really wonderful, well-trained CIA graduate, uh, Culinary Institute, who I learned so much from.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:37.790
 She was there for two, I was there for two weeks before she told me that she was leaving and was hoping I would take over her role as, um, bakery manager, which was a total shock to me.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:48.910
 And I did not feel equipped for that job at all, but yeah, it was kind of loaded right into my lap and sort of learned by fire and did that for a few years.

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:50.440
 And that was at Village Bakery?

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:50.840
 Mm-hmm.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:53.630
 And so at that time you were in graduate school?

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:54.850
 No, not yet.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:07:03.720
 I took several years off and, um, lived a life as a, as a afternoon painter, early morning baker.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:05.210
 Did both of them.

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:07.260
 What was that like?

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.960
 It was excellent.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.340
 I went to work at five.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:20.820
 I was off at one or two and had my afternoons to paint while the light was still wonderful and was kind of done, you know, at seven when it was dinnertime.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:25.490
 And yeah, yeah, it was good.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:26.690
 That was good.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.450
 And they went together really well.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:31.090
 I noticed they intermingled more and more.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:41.200
 The longer I did them together, there was this kind of beautiful precision process in baking that ended up following me into,

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:47.060
 the oil painting and vice versa, you know, mixing the frosting, started feeling like mixing the oil paint colors.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:59.620
 Even my application became very similar that I would be making these wedding cakes that were absolutely, um, sort of flaw-free.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:07.490
 They would be, you know, the frosting would be ladled on there and they would look like they were all one sheet of frosting, you know,

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:11.470
 just made perfect by a seamless touch.

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:19.510
 And then I wanted my canvases to look the same way, to layer the oil paint on over and over this very laborious process with a,

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:25.350
 with a big palette knife, you know, so that they ended up looking like they were frosted to total perfection.

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:27.210
 Yeah, it was funny.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:38.370
 Was that like, um, well, what was the first moment you felt that, um, synergy or that, that relatedness in craft?

00:08:41.770 --> 00:08:53.590
 Um, I think the first moment that it really stood out to me wasn't until a few years later when I was in grad school and it was actually pointed out to me.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:09:06.300
 Someone from the outside looking into my process said, oh, you, you treat both of these as medium and you handle them both the same way.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.000
 And I thought, that's so funny.

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:10.520
 That can't be true.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:19.290
 And that's sort of in retrospect, I looked at my whole last few years and realized that I did apply very similar, um,

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:31.390
 patience and skill and wonder to both of them, to painting and baking alike and, um, grew with both of them at the same time.

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:43.170
 So knowing you more as a painter than as a baker, you have this very, um, internal process.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:51.740
 I think about your painting, it's very, um, there's this really meditative quality to it, right?

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:59.350
 You, uh, you use color and form to create these patterns that feel very meditative.

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:02.650
 And you talk about your work like that in a lot of ways.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:14.970
 So does that, that, that to me feels like this very internal thing and was, is baking also an internal thing or like I'm interested in that process, that similarity.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:20.120
 Um, baking has this very latent physicality.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:29.600
 You don't really know that you're working so hard and pushing your limits so much until you're done with it and then you're exhausted.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:35.550
 And I think it sort of shares, it shares that quality with painting.

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:42.630
 Um, and I seem to love things that have this, this really arduous process in a way.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:51.180
 I find myself at the painting a lot of times creating a surface and just going at it and going at it and going at it till my hands are aching.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:55.030
 And baking's the same way, depending on what you have to do.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:11:04.730
 I mean, even just pushing the flour constantly through the sifter and moving the flour around and moving heavy bags and standing in front of a blazing oven.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:11.910
 Um, all of it's super physical, but you don't really know at the time that it's physical because it's so different than running.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:14.840
 It's so different than hiking.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:24.820
 You don't see the progress of it because you're basically in this little four foot square, just kind of running around, chasing yourself and mentally calculating constantly.

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:30.050
 Um, but I, I love that it exhausts me.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:32.760
 I love that both of the processes exhaust me.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:40.780
 And I don't know if I've ever really thought about that until, until you asked, you know.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:54.240
 So something, when you say that, I think, um, it's really, it's really trendy right now to talk about these explicit stories in food, the processes of food.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:04.360
 So where it comes from, who made it, less so how you eat it, and definitely not who cleans up after you, but the parts of the artisanal parts of food.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:14.180
 Um, when you talk about your process with painting and with baking, it seems like there's a lot of implicit story that goes into it.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:27.760
 And, um, maybe correct me if I'm wrong, but I get this distinct sense that you, that you, that you know there's a story there, that it is okay, that it's implicit.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:29.520
 It doesn't have to be obtuse.

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:34.150
 That feels very similar in both of those crafts in your presentation of them.

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:39.880
 Well, I get tired of my own voice, to be honest.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:58.220
 I teach, and in some ways when you're teaching, there is, there's so much outward communication, and there's so much technique that is explained and, and tried and retried.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:09.790
 And I really, I find a lot of respite in the places where I don't have to explain, where I don't have to reveal everything.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:12.890
 There's something really wonderful about that.

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:18.590
 Not even a consciously retained, but just a containment.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:24.020
 I think there's a certain containment around what the process is about for me.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:34.090
 Um, I'm happy to follow through if someone's curious, but I also know that I'm doing a lot of it for me.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:45.990
 And that a lot of my internal processing is actually just going straight into the object, whether it be food, or a painting, or a sculpture, or, um, or any other.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:50.120
 Object, um, I like that.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:14:02.680
 I, I, I like that there is a, a certain, uh, that they, it becomes a container for all that stuff rather than being explained so much that it dilutes its potency.

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:12.160
 I really love how you're describing that in the, that alchemical sort of nature.

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:13.500
 Yeah, exactly.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.120
 That mimics in both of those processes.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:22.900
 Um, I'd love to hear, though, about how your work exists in the world, right?

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:36.920
 So last year you were in this show at Yerba Buena Center for the Arts, which was a special curatorial project with Recreator Vanesha, um, called The Way Things Go.

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:43.380
 And that show was about migration, uh, amongst other things, when I looked it up online.

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.670
 I don't know what it was about, to be honest.

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:46.990
 Okay.

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:47.390
 Yeah.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:47.890
 Yeah.

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:50.030
 So, can you describe what you made for that?

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:51.190
 Talk about implicit, like.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:54.260
 I would, I would share.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.660
 I couldn't quite figure it out.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:56.620
 It was a good show.

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:58.540
 Yeah, thanks.

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:01.520
 Um, thanks for going to that show and checking it out.

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:07.060
 It, uh, Recreator is a very complicated man.

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:34.080
 Um, I was curated into a section that had to do, um, it was loosely called the Gourd Museum, but it mostly had to do with, um, container and something perhaps being food, perhaps being object, um, where it comes from and, and where it goes.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:52.690
 So, I think migration fits in there a little bit, food fits in there a little bit, um, I actually took more of the migration side of that conversation to create a piece that I made, um, that is a piece I've wanted to make for a very long time.

00:15:52.690 --> 00:16:03.200
 I wouldn't say I totally sidestepped the description, but I, I, I definitely went outside of Gourd itself.

00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:19.700
 Um, I made a piece that was, that started with this seed of information traces back to, um, the reservation that I grew up next to, which is the Dresslerville Reservation of Washoe Indians in Nevada.

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:30.320
 And I thought about their migration, how their place of residence was around Lake Tahoe, beautiful, serene, sort of perfect, wonderful place to live.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:36.060
 Maybe what they might have gone through to secure that place and to keep that place.

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:49.570
 And during this colonial conversation that happened, um, they were relegated to other dry areas, um, in this surrounding, like probably 50 miles radius around there.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:58.490
 And they were given back one tiny little resort on the lake, which is only really available during a certain part of the year.

00:16:58.610 --> 00:17:01.130
 So I was very interested in that migration.

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:07.490
 And, and when I was looking more at the Washoe Indians, they're in the middle of a project actually to raise money.

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:09.930
 Coincidentally, that is a gourd project.

00:17:10.090 --> 00:17:15.690
 They're trying to raise money with these beautiful painted gourds that they make to preserve their language.

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:20.100
 And I was thinking about what is lost in all this colonization.

00:17:21.100 --> 00:17:26.970
 And obviously they were telling me very clearly with this project that languages are lost, cultures are lost.

00:17:28.070 --> 00:17:49.920
 Um, and so I, so I started thinking about California, which is where I reside now and wanted to represent each tribe and the place where that tribe actually, uh, was located, was named for before colonization, before Western colonization happened.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:55.860
 And, and, um, so I made vessels, I made containers that represented each one of those places.

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:58.760
 There were 61 tribes in California.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:05.280
 I made 61 hand-built tiny little vessels that in my mind, they would hold one drink of water.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:15.720
 And I, um, I laid them out on a big piece of walnut that was sort of the shape of the state of California and, and put it up.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:27.650
 Um, I wanted to elevate it, put it up higher than the ground plane, wanted it kind of like more on the waist plane or stomach plane or where you would feel that drink of water when you took it in.

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:47.200
 Um, so the 61 vessels that were all gradient, different colors with the names of the tribes inside sat atop, uh, drawings of, uh, woven baskets that were made by the Washoe tribe, different members of the Washoe tribe that actually continued to make their livings weaving baskets.

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:50.390
 Once the areas were colonized.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:57.840
 I guess they ask about that piece because for me, it really elicited a feeling of a table.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:18:58.870
 Yeah.

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:01.430
 This, and a meal, right?

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:02.310
 It was an offering.

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:06.220
 So there were all these vessels and they were empty as they were presented.

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:16.570
 So somehow to me, that feels like that is related to, to feelings of nourishment or place or being fed or something.

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:19.490
 But they're not, I don't know what that is.

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:22.850
 Um, so when you showed the piece, there wasn't water.

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:24.900
 There was no water.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:35.430
 Actually, I was inviting, um, a reenactment of the colonization and, um, there were vessels.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:38.450
 There were 61 vessels also that were out in the foyer.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:54.150
 And as a participant visitor, you could take a white vessel into the main show and then remove one of the tribes and place a white vessel in place of the white, of the tribe and take the vet, take the tribe home with you.

00:19:54.480 --> 00:20:07.990
 So it, it ended up having less to do with water and more about reenactment and sort of reframing, um, reframing the act of taking and giving and representing.

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:19.980
 And, uh, more than anything, it was just sort of keeping the tribe in one's thoughts, bringing the tribe to one's house, to one's dwelling into one's, the forefront of one's thought.

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:28.690
 Almost like, I mean, it, it, it ended up looking like a table though, very much so.

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:33.270
 And I thought I liked the idea that it ended up looking domestic.

00:20:35.100 --> 00:20:35.220
 Yeah.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:38.060
 It just really elicited this feeling of hospitality.

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:38.980
 Mm-hmm.

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:46.500
 And kind of the discomfort of hospitality of giving and taking and what's on the table or what's not on the table for taking.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.390
 So I think it was really successful in those ways.

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:50.190
 Oh, thanks.

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:50.450
 Yeah.

00:20:52.030 --> 00:21:01.880
 Well, so let's talk about some other ways that you've engaged with food or baking and your, and your art practice.

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:23.520
 Um, I want to talk a little bit about the parking lot art fair that we did together, um, which I've, in, in my estimation was like an experiment into the place you actually want to be at the art fair, which is by the coffee with something good to eat.

00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:33.110
 Um, but I just wanted to hear what your conception of that project was and maybe where it went for you.

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:36.020
 That was a really fun project to work on.

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:46.190
 By the way, um, it was such a wonderful way kind of to get to know you outside of the way that I knew you to kind of come together as collaborators.

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:51.690
 And, um, also in a way I felt like we were just throwing caution to the wind.

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:58.970
 We're going to come up with a loose concept here, sweet, salty, bitter, sour, and see where it goes.

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:10.310
 Um, and the fact that we just sort of brought flavors in, instead of creating this, you know, huge conceptual thing or visual thing.

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:29.060
 For me, it was just about bringing flavors and sharing over something very basic, sharing with people so that the visitors became participants in that sharing and accepting of something.

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:46.040
 And what I noticed at that fair was that, you know, people came to us, they, they didn't ask much what we were doing, but they saw that there was coffee and biscuits and jam and, and we're friendly.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:47.580
 They weren't overwhelmed by it.

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:48.540
 They were welcomed.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:23:05.120
 And I noticed right away that I, um, I had been sorely lacking that kind of easy conversation with art, that sort of conversation that you get over food and drink that is sort of just celebratory and jovial.

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:19.960
 Um, and, um, um, my big takeaway, other than just working with you and having fun doing it along with all the other, all the other participants was just that I liked the quality of that conversation and that exchange.

00:23:20.130 --> 00:23:39.810
 Um, and I wanted more of it, um, I wanted more of that feeling of, I don't know, this sort of like solidarity over nourishment, you know, it felt so simple and kind of like, um, untinted by anything else.

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:44.170
 Yeah. And so what happened next?

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:56.410
 Well, so that seemed so fun that I realized I wanted to make more biscuits.

00:23:56.410 --> 00:24:14.260
 I had made some biscuits for that and I wanted to come up with the perfect biscuit because it was just a fun chemistry experiment for me to come up with something that was, um, crunchy and light and sweet and delicious and kept you wanting more and slightly addictive.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:18.870
 And friendly and always ready at the right time.

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:23.090
 And so we started baking and baking and baking every day.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:28.140
 And my neighbors were very excited about that because I always had biscuits to bring to them.

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:35.790
 Um, and then I thought, why couldn't I do this, spend the summer doing this?

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:38.380
 My teaching assignment was almost over.

00:24:38.540 --> 00:24:46.400
 It was the end of spring and I was really holding on to that idea of the easy communication,

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:48.650
 this gift of food.

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:53.660
 And part of it had to do with the fact that I live in two places.

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:57.770
 I live halftime in Oakland and halftime in the forest and Occidental.

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:04.260
 And so community for me becomes, um, a patchwork in a way.

00:25:04.380 --> 00:25:11.260
 I'm with some community, sometimes some other times and was feeling more like more of my time was in Oakland.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:18.760
 I wanted to connect more during the summer with my Sonoma County people and maybe people I didn't know in Sonoma County.

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:21.280
 Just kind of find out what was happening.

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:24.600
 All these thoughts were going on all at once, but the biscuits were good.

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:26.470
 The experiments went well.

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:36.080
 I started imagining what you can do with biscuits, making, um, making offerings of different kinds of biscuits, different combinations.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.560
 I just was getting excited about it.

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:52.490
 And then kind of just decided to start a pop-up in the style of maybe like social, almost like social activism, like Meryl Ukele's, where I would make food and bring it to a spot where they didn't have it.

00:25:52.490 --> 00:25:55.010
 And exchange over that food.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:26:10.220
 And I wanted it to be, if I was going to sell it, which I'd have to, I wanted it to be very, very affordable and, um, local so that everyone was participating, um, and friendly.

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:13.590
 And it all kind of came together at once.

00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:14.710
 It was amazing.

00:26:14.990 --> 00:26:19.430
 And in just a few weeks that I wanted to address the fact that I felt nomadic.

00:26:19.750 --> 00:26:24.950
 So I called it Hello Nomad, being that I would greet people on their nomadic journey as well.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:34.390
 I made a little pastry case, which was a complete joy to fabricate, um, out of all just found stuff in my basement.

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:38.670
 Um, and it fit perfectly into the hatch of my car.

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:44.570
 So I pulled open the hatch, pulled out the pastry case, brought as many biscuits as I could make.

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:48.990
 And my friend at the time, his, he and his little 10-year-old were roasting coffee.

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:51.570
 So they sold me coffee really inexpensively.

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:55.990
 And so I had fresh roasted beans and coffee and these biscuits.

00:26:56.190 --> 00:27:01.410
 And I wanted people to be able to, you know, buy it with the money in their pocket.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:04.870
 Very easy, affordable, and where you want to be.

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:11.730
 So I set up in these kind of obscure places, like this little tiny firehouse in Camp Meeker in the forest.

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:17.890
 And then, um, I was invited to set up outside of, um, Sonoma County Meat Company,

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:22.370
 which is in sort of a strange post-industrial little area.

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:26.610
 Um, and yeah, that was super fun.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:29.610
 Then outside of Kitty Hawk Gallery, I set up.

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:33.430
 And it just, it became this thing that had its own life.

00:27:33.690 --> 00:27:40.130
 And every time I would work on a Tuesday or Wednesday, I would bring the leftovers back from, um, uh,

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:43.170
 the guys who pick up my recycling and garbage.

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:47.010
 I would always roll in about the same time that they were driving past.

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:49.740
 So then it became this other thing as well.

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:53.670
 And I don't know, that's, that's kind of the evolution of it.

00:27:53.670 --> 00:28:03.240
 I'm really interested in the, the part of this intersection of like, you have a job that's a seasonal job, right?

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:05.720
 And so you have summers off because you teach.

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:06.260
 Yeah.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:14.640
 And that also, um, means you need something to do in the summer, whether it's being working in the studio

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:16.660
 or some other kind of project like that.

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:17.740
 It's that kind of time.

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:25.090
 And, um, it seems like this whole year has really been in some ways like an investigation of place

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:32.210
 where you've really been thinking a lot about where you live and where you don't live part of the time.

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:35.510
 And, you know, I mean, kind of that back and forth, that nomadic thing.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:39.980
 And what was it like to have an excuse to talk to people?

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:43.930
 It was pretty thrilling, to be honest.

00:28:44.210 --> 00:28:48.820
 I, I feel removed from my community in Sonoma County quite often.

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:55.550
 And I leave at five in the morning and come back and it's dinnertime and, you know, you

00:28:55.550 --> 00:29:00.170
 have responsibilities at night, especially I'm, I have a 14 year old, so there are big

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:00.910
 responsibilities.

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:07.030
 And so although I live here, I feel like I only live here and I don't think this is, um,

00:29:07.030 --> 00:29:08.090
 at all unique to me.

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:13.860
 This is kind of, this is the standard commuter kind of woe.

00:29:14.060 --> 00:29:19.980
 So for me to be out there and engaging with people that I don't regularly see that I live,

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:25.160
 you know, 20 minutes from 30 minutes from was absolutely thrilling.

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:30.910
 The fact that I be like the pop-up became a destination and I was able to engage with

00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:37.830
 people regularly over their coffee and biscuit in the morning to be up and around in the morning

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:42.810
 and be in my community in the morning rather than on the road was amazing.

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:51.660
 I was, um, given this great gift of getting to know people, getting to know them better rather

00:29:51.660 --> 00:29:53.250
 than just know who they were.

00:29:53.250 --> 00:29:58.460
 I actually got to know them, got to sit and talk with them, got to hear what they were

00:29:58.460 --> 00:30:01.940
 thinking about first thing in the morning, which I think are always some, they're some

00:30:01.940 --> 00:30:03.540
 of the most amazing thoughts in the world.

00:30:03.680 --> 00:30:04.820
 They're the dreamers thoughts.

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:11.320
 And it was great because people were coming and hanging out that they were coming and meeting

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:11.820
 friends.

00:30:11.820 --> 00:30:14.220
 They were bringing their kids, they were bringing their dogs.

00:30:14.910 --> 00:30:19.160
 And, um, I don't know.

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:19.840
 It was amazing.

00:30:20.200 --> 00:30:21.510
 It was a ton of work.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:23.230
 I was exhausted by it.

00:30:24.370 --> 00:30:30.630
 And, um, it was also just totally delightful.

00:30:30.970 --> 00:30:32.190
 There were so many things about it.

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:35.760
 So many aspects of this giant experiment.

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:41.800
 All I can say is that it was wonderful and I feel more connected with my community than

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:42.900
 I could have imagined.

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:47.360
 Uh, and it continues to grow, even though I'm back to teaching now.

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:55.910
 I still feel this wonderful shadow of hello nomad and planning what its next incarnation will

00:30:55.910 --> 00:30:56.610
 look like.

00:30:57.110 --> 00:31:05.190
 And I know that it, it in some way created this very nice residue in the community because

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:06.790
 I'm asked about it all the time.

00:31:07.660 --> 00:31:10.740
 My neighbor actually for my birthday made me a new pastry case.

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:15.910
 He's a woodworker and it's very shiny and lovely, much better than the one I made.

00:31:16.090 --> 00:31:21.850
 And that made me believe that he didn't want me to end the business, you know, so it just

00:31:21.850 --> 00:31:22.490
 kept going.

00:31:23.170 --> 00:31:25.700
 Um, I learned a lot.

00:31:26.470 --> 00:31:29.290
 I learned a lot about how much is actually happening where I live.

00:31:29.690 --> 00:31:37.370
 When before I thought it was pretty sleepy, there is a lot of artistic activity of so many

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:39.210
 crafts people live around here.

00:31:39.330 --> 00:31:45.790
 So many do it yourselfers and farmers and, um, got to talk to a lot of service people in

00:31:45.790 --> 00:31:51.700
 the morning that would be happening by or people, you know, truck drivers that would stop by

00:31:51.700 --> 00:31:55.970
 to do whatever they were doing in the morning, got to talk to them.

00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:58.310
 And, uh, it was very nourishing.

00:31:59.740 --> 00:32:02.280
 Can you, was it an art project?

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:04.530
 That I don't know.

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:11.230
 What I was very certain about though, was it started in my mind conceptually like an art

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:11.730
 project.

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:18.190
 Um, because, you know, by default, I think it's just the way that I think is in this way that's

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:21.330
 been labeled art project because it's process oriented.

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:30.960
 Um, what I found that that was really nice for me was that there was no intimidation factor

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:37.810
 with food was that people spoke a very everyday utilitarian language about food.

00:32:37.810 --> 00:32:46.970
 They express their satisfaction or desire or needs or wants, or I don't know, clear observations,

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:53.170
 um, where I knew that these same people I had interacted with in galleries.

00:32:53.670 --> 00:33:00.730
 And there was, there was some other kind of, uh, filter there.

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:02.790
 Then it wasn't an easy communication.

00:33:02.790 --> 00:33:04.810
 Food is an easy communication.

00:33:05.120 --> 00:33:08.440
 It's either yummy or it's not.

00:33:08.560 --> 00:33:11.390
 It's either satisfying a need or it's not.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:14.230
 They either want more of it or they don't.

00:33:14.890 --> 00:33:17.170
 Um, and I love that.

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:25.520
 That was really exciting to me to think that, wow, this is food, but it also might be art

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:27.240
 and it also might be good design.

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.800
 It also might be totally process-based.

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:31.560
 It might be conceptual.

00:33:32.490 --> 00:33:39.410
 Um, it might be all these things, but in the end, it's kind of just a wonderful sense enough

00:33:39.410 --> 00:33:40.070
 of ordinary.

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:42.680
 If that makes sense.

00:33:43.220 --> 00:33:47.930
 It was ordinary enough for everyone to engage in it.

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:54.780
 That's such a nice way to begin to wrap up our conversation.

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:56.980
 I just want to stay there in that moment.

00:33:57.760 --> 00:33:59.480
 Um, what happens next with it?

00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:01.470
 What's...

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:08.610
 Well, um, uh, since I started teaching again this fall semester, I've done a couple of,

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:12.400
 um, events, private events at, at folks' houses.

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:17.180
 Um, but the odd thing is that they have also been mixed art events too.

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:25.150
 Um, and I like now that it's starting to overlap this biscuit baking and art business.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:33.710
 Um, I am planning for the spring to start, start it up again, start this mobile business.

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:41.240
 And I'm looking at a teardrop trailer right now to be the little mobile nomad unit.

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:48.460
 Um, but I also have, you know, a million art ideas on the burner as well.

00:34:49.470 --> 00:34:57.980
 So it's, it's going to be a delicate balance trying to figure out what goes where in this whole puzzle.

00:35:00.350 --> 00:35:00.550
 Yeah.

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:04.750
 That seems like that's, that's been your balance for a long time.

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:05.190
 Yeah.

00:35:06.170 --> 00:35:08.930
 May the journey continue with the crazy balance.

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:10.200
 Yeah.

00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:14.000
 We'll see what falls off, you know, what, what, what doesn't want to keep rolling.

00:35:14.910 --> 00:35:17.360
 Well, I think I should wrap it up there.

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:20.380
 Thanks for talking to me about your projects.

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:21.800
 Thanks so much, Chelsea.

